## qbspserver: incremental `qbsp` — speed report #2

`qbspserver` keeps a map loaded in the background and recompiles it when you run `qbspreceive` (e.g. from your editor's compile script), redoing only what your edit touched. The output must **play the same** as `qbsp`, and every benchmark run checks it: entities, surfaces per texture, alignment, and hull 0/1/2 contents at ~46k points.

**Benchmark** (9 edit scenarios × 2 maps, median of 3, paired with plain `qbsp`; Core 7 240H laptop):
```
                       e3m4 (LibreQuake)   E1M1
plain qbsp                  ~5.8 s         ~1.0 s
qbspserver, no change       0.05 s         0.03 s
  1 entity / 1 texture      0.6 s          0.17 s
  1 brush moved / added     1.3 s          0.31 s
  many edits (2% brushes)   2.8 s          0.45 s
```
**All scenarios: 13.3 s, 4.6× faster than plain qbsp** (first report: 18.7 s).

**Big map** (quakemash, 35k brushes; qbsp was 271 s): texture edit **5.0 s**, entity **6.0 s**, brush moved **10.8 s**.

**How:** clipping hulls reused whole on texture/entity edits; on geometry edits rebuilt only in the 1024-unit grid cells you touched; hull 0's tree reused when the brushes didn't change.

**Bonus for everyone: plain `qbsp` is 3.2× faster** (quakemash 271 → 84 s), with **byte-identical** BSP and .prt. Some finds:
• The split plane choice re-scanned every brush side per candidate, on one thread at the top of the tree (4 s for one node).
• `std::min({a, b, c})` in the bounds code: MSVC makes it a vectorized library call for 3 values. **14%** of the compile.
• A `std::list` node per portal fragment: 12-16%.
• Windows 11 throttles "background" processes (EcoQoS): 25% slower from an editor. This fork opts out.

Next: map loading (1 s per compile on big maps), hulls built at the same time.
